A: The solution that worked for me was different and quite simple:


*

*Copy-and-Paste the entire class file. You mentioned in your original question you are working in two classes but are using the same setup here. That is likely causing the problem. I'm guessing this is a common issue, especially when working with IDEs that often have this feature.

*Replace all the existing code in the second class with the provided one and save it.

*Run the tests again and you should be able to prove your contracts and invariants for your new class.  


You need two class files if you want to test a different class but it seems you are working with the same class twice which could be the issue.

A: I apologize for the confusion, but it seems this is not an answer per se but a kind of way to explain what is likely causing the issue.

It seems you didn't create two separate tests for your ArrayListContractTest class. There might be some problem of sharing the original test file between two ArrayListContractTest classes. When you copied the content and replaced the original one, only the second test wasn’t created.

To confirm the issue, consider:


*

*Create a test method in the second class with the same name and signature as the originally correct method. Verify that the PUT_GetContract test case fails with a non-match.

*If the "Non-Match" error is still a problem, consider renaming one of the methods after each execution. That would allow you to compare the behavior of methods in the two classes separately.

*Also consider commenting out the problematic test cases in your original test file and re-running as a separate test to see if the problem is isolated to the current class tests.

*If this doesn't resolve the issue, consider looking at how you initialized & called the ArrayList class in your tests. The PexSettings may need to be explicitly set for each new test case or method.

*In the PexSettings set the UnderTestMethod attribute to both test classes.

*Consider checking the Pex assembly references. If there are multiple, it might cause conflict.


This is just a suggestion, and it may need a different perspective to solve the issue.

A: When the code base becomes complex and contains many projects I've found using a version control system like Git can be very helpful. Git allows you to keep track of changes as you make them, revert your code when it doesn't work as expected, and create branches for new work or testing. 

Microsoft has a Visual Studio extension called GitHub extension that is compatible with Visual Studio, and it's quite user-friendly. Here is a brief walkthrough on how to setup Git integration with Visual Studio:


*

*Go to Extensions > Manage Extensions in Visual Studio, and then search for 'GitHub' to find the GitHub extension for Visual Studio.

*Click Install to install the GitHub extension.

*Once installed, right click on your project and select Git > Initialize Repository to initialize a Git repository in your project.

*Then go to the Source Control > Advanced, where you can enter a description and click on Commit. This commits your changes with a message for what they have done.


After this setup, each "commit" of your project (or each change/testing you make) becomes a new version, which becomes useful for tracking and comparing changes.

I hope this helps, and happy coding =)

A: I've experienced similar issue due to duplicate TestMethods with the same signature in the same test class. Test discovery is based on method name and parameter types. Try to recheck the parameters and method names in your test methods in the second class.

